# Cache policy for the landing page.
#
# Honoured by the Cloudflare deploy (`wrangler.jsonc` serves `dist/` as
# assets); Vite copies this file into `dist/` verbatim.
#
# Before this the site served everything with `max-age=14400` and no
# `immutable`, so a returning visitor revalidated the fingerprinted bundle
# every four hours. The rule below splits by *whether the name can change*:
#
#   /assets/*   Vite fingerprints every file it emits (index-<hash>.js,
#               demo-light-<hash>.webm, …), so the bytes behind a URL never
#               change and the browser may keep them for a year without
#               asking. This is where almost all of the page's weight lives.
#   public/*    Files copied verbatim keep their plain names
#               (favicon-32.png, apple-touch-icon.png, syscity.png) — a name
#               that can be replaced by a new upload must stay revalidatable,
#               so they get a day rather than a year.
#   the page    The HTML is the one document that must not go stale; it
#               carries the hashed URLs of everything else. Ten minutes is
#               what it had, kept deliberately short.

/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/*.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400

/*.svg
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400

/*.ico
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400

/
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=600

/index.html
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=600
